  "prompt": "[system]\n\n    You generate compact, robust Python TSP metaheuristics.\n    You must follow the requested interface exactly, always preserve tour validity.\n    \n\n[user]\nPARENTS:\nCandidate cand_000008: regret_insertion_farthest_start\n\nStatus: valid; distance: 17.8458\n\nThought: Based on regret insertion heuristic but initializes tour with the pair of cities having the maximum distance. Then iteratively insert remaining cities using regret (second-best minus best insertion cost) tie-broken by smaller best cost. Budget limited steps, falls back to arbitrary insertion.\n\nHistory source: yes\nHistory bucket: tsp:size:500\nHistory role: regime specialist\n\nCode:\n\n```python\n\nimport numpy as np\n\ndef solve_tsp(distance_matrix: np.ndarray, seed: int, budget: int) -> np.ndarray:\n    n = distance_matrix.shape[0]\n    if n <= 2:\n        return np.arange(n)\n    rng = np.random.RandomState(seed)\n    if budget < n:\n        tour = list(range(n))\n        rng.shuffle(tour)\n        report_best_tour(tour)\n        return np.array(tour)\n    # Find pair with maximum distance\n    max_dist = -1\n    pair = (0, 1)\n    for i in range(n):\n        for j in range(i+1, n):\n            if distance_matrix[i][j] > max_dist:\n                max_dist = distance_matrix[i][j]\n                pair = (i, j)\n    tour = list(pair)\n    remaining = set(range(n)) - set(pair)\n    steps_left = budget - n + 2\n    while remaining:\n        if steps_left <= 0:\n            tour.extend(sorted(remaining))\n            break\n        best_regret = -1\n        best_city = None\n        best_pos = None\n        best_cost = None\n        for city in remaining:\n            costs = []\n            cur_len = len(tour)\n            for p in range(cur_len + 1):\n                left = tour[p-1] if p > 0 else tour[-1]\n                right = tour[p] if p < cur_len else tour[0]\n                cost = (distance_matrix[left, city] + distance_matrix[city, right] -\n                        distance_matrix[left, right])\n                costs.append(cost)\n            sorted_costs = sorted(costs)\n            best = sorted_costs[0]\n            second_best = sorted_costs[1] if len(sorted_costs) > 1 else best\n            regret = second_best - best\n            if regret > best_regret or (regret == best_regret and best < best_cost):\n                best_regret = regret\n                best_city = city\n                best_pos = costs.index(best)\n                best_cost = best\n        tour = tour[:best_pos] + [best_city] + tour[best_pos:]\n        remaining.remove(best_city)\n        steps_left -= 1\n    report_best_tour(tour)\n    return np.array(tour)\n\n```\n\nVERBAL GRADIENT: follow the reflection below as the primary source of change.\n\nUse the supplied parents as supporting evidence only.\n\nPARENT-SPECIFIC LLM REFLECTIONS:\n\nParent cand_000008 LLM reflection:\n- Summary: The candidate, using a max-distance pair start, yielded a slightly worse tour length (17.846) than its parent (17.793), indicating the starting pair choice may degrade quality.\n- Aim: Add a local-search phase (e.g., 2-opt) after construction, using remaining budget to perform neighbor evaluations and reporting the best tour found.\n- Preserve: regret insertion logic; budget handling (steps_left fallback); valid permutation guarantee\n- Change: Insert a 2-opt improvement loop that iterates until budget exhausted or no improvement; Periodically call report_best_tour during local search\n- Avoid: Altering the insertion heuristic or tie-breaking; Removing budget checks; Introducing invalid tours\n\nDistance is the search objective for TSP; lower distance is better.\n\nImplement this function only:\n\ndef solve_tsp(distance_matrix: np.ndarray, seed: int, budget: int) -> np.ndarray:\n\nRequirements:\n- Minimize total TSP tour distance.\n- Return a 1D np.ndarray containing each node exactly once.\n- Do not repeat the start node at the end.\n- Always return a valid tour, even when budget is very small.\n- Use only distance_matrix; do not assume coordinates.\n- Use seed for randomness.\n- Treat budget as a hard cap on search effort.\n- Create a valid tour early.\n- Call report_best_tour(tour) whenever a new best valid tour is found, assume this func already exists.\n- Do not use files, network, subprocesses, or external solvers.\n- Keep the code compact and robust.\n\ncorrect signature, valid tour on every return path,\nearly report_best_tour, budget-bounded main search, allowed imports only, no I/O/network/subprocesses.\n\nReturn one JSON object and nothing else:\n\n{\n  \"name\": \"short_snake_case_name\",\n  \"thought\": \"high-level summary of the construction, improvement, and tie-break logic\",\n  \"code\": \"complete Python code as a JSON string\"\n}\nNo Markdown, fences, or text outside JSON. The code string must define solve_tsp.",
